  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm front will continue to limp north thru the forecast area.\nMid to high level cloudiness is likely to linger north of that \nboundary thru sunrise. That will keep things warmer than low\ntemps yesterday. I do think that despite some clouds, radiation \nfog should still develop in the major river valleys by sun up.\nThe earlier surge of warm advection rain/showers that tried to \nenter the forecast area ended up being primarily virga. Any \nshowers moving east out of central and North Country New York \nwill also struggle against dry air. So I may favor sprinkles \nover measurable precip in that case. In the warm sector today \ntemps will surge well into and thru the 70s in some cases.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nOver the last 24 hours ensemble guidance has shifted pretty\nsizable way on rain chances for the remainder of the week. The \ntwo events have largely flipped, with Thu trending towards more \nQPF and Sun trending drier. This is generally true of both the \nGEFS and ECMWF Ens, but the GEFS remains somewhat bearish on Thu\nstill as well. Looking at the latest NBM guidance, it has come \nup for Thu, including introducing some categorical PoP. It has \nalso backed off PoP over the weekend. This is my preferred shift\nto the forecast, so at this time no changes to the NBM are \nplanned. \n\nOne of the reasons that the weekend rain chances have backed\noff is that guidance is coming in with stronger high pressure\nbuilding down from central Canada. This is suppressing rain and\nthe surface boundary south. ECMWF EFI is favoring Fri to be\nbreezy behind a cold front as the high initially builds in. That\nwill set up both Sat and Sun to be cooler days. Right now NBM\ntemps are 5 to 10 degrees cooler than normal during the day.\nOvernight will also be cool, and given the time of year that\nmeans some frost potential across the northern valleys.",
